from common import lesson, lab, cite, transcript

LESSONS = [
lesson(3,1,'max-activating-examples','Max-activating examples and why they mislead',
 'The top-k view is where every investigation starts and where most wrong explanations are born. Quantile sampling is the fix.',
 '''<h2 id="top-k">What the top-k view shows</h2>
<p>Take neuron 2928 in layer 2 of GPT-2 small, run the course corpus through the model, and sort the 3,200 tokens by the neuron’s activation. The ten largest, from the authoring run:</p>
'''+transcript('''layer 2 neuron 2928: 3200 tokens; activation mean 0.033, sd 0.417, max 2.103; fraction > 0: 0.169; fraction > 0.5*max: 0.0706

Top 10 activations (what a max-activating-examples view shows):
    2.103   boiled the kettle.[\\n\\n]\\nHOW
    2.037   delay the sailing.[\\n\\n]\\nA
    1.905   about a clock.[ Both] customers are
    1.865   think probably true.[ But] section 3
    1.854   predict.\\n\\n[Second], the
    1.847   into the ground.[ So]akaways
    1.822   two routes agree.[\\n]\\nWhen
    1.798   is for delay.[\\n\\n]\\nSH
    1.786  's always clicked."[\\n]\\n"
    1.780   years.\\n\\n\\n[NOT]ES T''')+'''
<p>The bracketed token is the one whose activation is shown. Every one of the ten follows a full stop or a paragraph break. A human looking at this writes “fires at the start of a sentence,” and that is a good hypothesis. It is also exactly the kind of evidence that Gao’s post warns about: “Top activations can often give a misleading impression of how monosemantic a neurons is” '''+cite('gao2026')+'''. Three distinct things can go wrong.</p>
<h2 id="three-failures">Three ways the top-k view misleads</h2>
<p><strong>Selection bias.</strong> The ten tokens were chosen because they are extreme. Any neuron, including one with no coherent function, has ten most extreme tokens, and with a vocabulary of 50,257 and a corpus of a few thousand tokens, the extremes of a noisy unit will often share a superficial feature by chance. The top-k view cannot distinguish a real rule from the tail of a distribution.</p>
<p><strong>Polysemanticity hidden in the tail.</strong> Suppose the neuron fires on sentence starts <em>and</em> on something else at half the strength. The second thing never reaches the top ten. The Curve Detectors paper is explicit that its quilts of examples across the activation range exist for this reason: “Quilts of images reveal patterns across a wide range of activations, but they can be misleading” '''+cite('curves')+'''. Their 3b:379 “fires in just 11% of cases across the dataset,” and most of those firings are weak.</p>
<p><strong>The top-k illusion of sufficiency.</strong> Even if every top example fits the pattern, the pattern may not be sufficient: Gao’s footnote puts it as “X matches Y is not commutative!” The neuron may fire on all the top sentence starts and still be silent on most sentence starts. Only a test that scores the pattern on every token, including the ones where the neuron is quiet, can tell.</p>
<h2 id="quantiles">Quantile sampling</h2>
<p>The fix is to look at random examples from fixed bands of the activation distribution instead of the extreme. The same script prints four bands; here are the first two and the last:</p>
'''+transcript('''Random tokens from activation quantile bands (what the top-k view hides):
  band 0.99-1.00 (activation 1.660 to 2.103, 32 tokens):
      1.822   two routes agree.[\\n]\\nWhen
      1.758   her a measurement.[\\n]\\nShe
      1.716   story were false.[ If] the ch
      1.672   responding to that.[ You] need the
  band 0.90-0.99 (activation 0.551 to 1.660, 288 tokens):
      1.518   and a remark.[ The] remarks are
      1.499   bought it."\\n[\\n]"It
      1.257   in September.\\n[\\n]The harvest
      0.606   listening on Tuesday.[ Those] are different
  band 0.00-0.50 (activation -0.170 to -0.118, 1600 tokens):
     -0.133   the control that would[ fail] if your
     -0.157   years. It read[ high] by about
     -0.164   on the phone and[ didn]'t notice
     -0.168  THE HARBOUR[ LED]GER\\n''')+'''
<p>For this neuron the picture holds up in the bands: the 90th to 99th percentile is still sentence starts, and the bottom half is mid-sentence tokens. That is reassuring but it is still looking, not testing. Lesson 04 scores the rule on every held-out token, and the sentence-start rule gets a held-out correlation of 0.845 against a random-neuron 95th percentile of 0.125. The looking suggested the rule; the scoring is what demonstrates it.</p>
<p>Two things to read off the summary line above the top ten. The neuron is positive on 16.9% of tokens but above half its maximum on only 7.1%, so a rule that predicts “fires” for 10% of tokens is in the right regime. And the mean activation is 0.033 with standard deviation 0.417, which is what a sparse unit looks like: a correlation between a 0/1 rule and this distribution is dominated by whether the rule finds the 7% of strong tokens, which is why lesson 04 also reports the base rate beside every score.</p>
<h2 id="essay">What the 2026 essay does with weak activations</h2>
<p>Cammarata’s essay reads the whole distribution rather than the top, and reports that weak activations change the picture: “How far to go into weak activations is a big factor here,” with the Qwen3-32B audit’s own caveat that the share of neurons with one job “varies depending on how you set the threshold for counting weak activations, depth, density of sub-circuits, and what you view as ‘one thing’” '''+cite('cammarata2026')+'''. Gao’s reply puts weight on the same region for the opposite reason: “My best guess from all the results I've seen is that ablating small neuron activations quite substantially degrades model performance, so I don’t think we get to ignore weak activations completely” '''+cite('gao2026')+'''. Both agree you must look below the top. They disagree about whether the weak region needs to be predicted to count as understood. The course’s position is procedural: decide before you look which region your score covers, and say so.</p>
<div class="evals"><span class="label">In interpretability work</span><p>Before you write what a unit does, print the activation summary (fraction positive, fraction above half max) and one random example from each quantile band. If the bands disagree with the top ten, you have two hypotheses, not one.</p></div>
<div class="pitfall"><span class="label">Pitfall</span><p>Mistaking a dataset artefact for a neuron property. The corpus here is 3,270 tokens of prose with many paragraph breaks. A neuron that looks like a “paragraph break” neuron on this corpus may look different on code. Held-out data from the same distribution tests generalisation within the distribution; it does not test the distribution.</p></div>''',
 'A unit’s top 20 examples are all chemical formulas. Random samples from its 50th–90th percentile band are mostly ordinary nouns with a few formulas. What are the two hypotheses, and what single number from lesson 04 would separate them?',
 'Hypothesis A: a formula detector with a long weak tail of unrelated firing. Hypothesis B: a broader unit (say, “technical noun”) whose extreme is formulas. The held-out correlation of each rule with the activations separates them: if “is a formula” scores much higher than “is a technical noun” across all tokens, A; if the broader rule scores higher, B. The top-20 view cannot tell, because both hypotheses predict it.',
 lab(['python labs/reference/maxact.py --layer 0 --neuron 908','python labs/reference/maxact.py --layer 9 --neuron 788'],
 'Run the quantile view on two neurons the screening in lesson 04 will pick: L0/908 and L9/788. Before running, write what rule you expect from the first band alone; then check the lower bands.',
 'L0/908 is the digit neuron and L9/788 the punctuation neuron from lesson 04’s table. Layer-0 neurons tend to be token-identity units whose bands agree; deeper ones mix context in. Whatever you see, the question for lesson 04 is the same: what does the rule score on tokens you did not look at?'), ['gao2026','curves','cammarata2026']),

lesson(4,1,'scoring-by-prediction','Scoring an explanation by held-out activation prediction',
 'Turn an explanation into predicted activations, score the predictions against the real ones on held-out tokens, and read the number for what it is.',
 '''<h2 id="simulation">The explain–simulate–score loop</h2>
<p>Bills et al. (2023) made the idea operational at scale. A language model writes an explanation of a neuron from its top-activating excerpts; a second model, given only the explanation, simulates the neuron’s activation on each token of new excerpts; the simulation is scored against the truth. They call the result an “explanation score”: “a measure of a language model's ability to compress and reconstruct neuron activations using natural language” '''+cite('bills2023')+'''. The score is a correlation: “If ρ is the correlation coefficient between the true and simulated activations, then we scale simulations so their mean matches that of the true activations, and their standard deviation is ρ times the standard deviation of the true activations. This maximizes explained variance at ρ².”</p>
<p>You do not need a language model to run this loop. The course’s cheap deterministic variant replaces the simulator with a rule: a regex or a predicate on the token and its predecessor that outputs 1 where the explanation says the neuron fires and 0 elsewhere. The score is the Pearson correlation between that 0/1 series and the activations on held-out tokens. It is cruder than a simulator that predicts magnitudes, and it is completely reproducible, which is what you want while learning.</p>
<div class="math">r = Σ(aᵢ − ā)(pᵢ − p̄) / √[Σ(aᵢ − ā)² · Σ(pᵢ − p̄)²]</div>
<p>Here aᵢ is the neuron’s activation on held-out token i, pᵢ is the rule’s prediction (0 or 1), and the bars are means over the held-out tokens. r is 1 if the neuron fires exactly where the rule says and nowhere else, with constant strength; it is 0 if the rule and the neuron are unrelated.</p>
<h2 id="run">The run</h2>
<p><code>labs/reference/neuron_score.py</code> documents its procedure in its docstring and prints it. The corpus is tokenised and cut into 128-token chunks; even chunks are the screening split, odd chunks are held out. For the rule “token is a digit,” the script searches every neuron in every layer (12 × 3,072 = 36,864 candidates) for the best correlation on the screening split, then scores the winner once on the held-out split beside two controls.</p>
'''+transcript('''corpus: 3270 tokens -> 13 screening chunks, 12 held-out chunks of 128
pre-registered: held-out r >= 0.5 and above the 95th percentile of 200 random same-layer neurons and of 500 label shuffles

         rule layer neuron  train r held-out r  rand95  shuf95 |rand|max  base rate  verdict
        digit     0    908    0.979      0.978   0.116   0.040     0.334      0.029  demonstrated''')+'''
<p>Read it column by column. The screening correlation (0.979) is the maximum over 36,864 searches and is not evidence by itself. The held-out correlation (0.978) is the evidence; it barely dropped, which tells you the rule generalises within this corpus. The random-neuron 95th percentile (0.116) says that a typical other neuron in layer 0 scores around 0.1 against the digit rule, and the largest of 200 random neurons scored 0.334; the shuffled-label 95th percentile (0.040) says a meaningless rule on this neuron scores near zero. The base rate, 2.9% of held-out tokens are digits, bounds how much any 0/1 rule can explain of a sparse unit. The verdict applies the pre-registered rule: r ≥ 0.5 and above both control percentiles.</p>
<h2 id="which-score">Which score, on which data</h2>
<p>The number depends on the excerpts it is computed on, and Bills et al. are candid about this. Their headline figure, “over 1,000 neurons with explanations that scored at least 0.8,” is qualified in the same sentence: “meaning that according to GPT-4 they account for most of the neuron's top-activating behavior.” Their scoring used a “top-and-random” mixture, which “can be thought of as an explanation’s ability to capture the neuron’s most strongly represented feature (from the top text excerpts), with a penalty for overly broad explanations (from the random text excerpts)” '''+cite('bills2023')+'''. Huang et al. (2023) showed what that mixture lets through: “The GPT-4 score is computed on a set of 10 examples from the GPT-2 XL training corpus, 5 containing tokens with top activations and 5 randomly sampled,” and they construct an unfaithful explanation that scores perfectly with high probability. Auditing 300 of the explanations with score at least 0.8, they found “a mean F1 score of 0.56 (with a precision of 0.64 and a recall of 0.50), whereas the random baseline has a F1 score of zero” '''+cite('huang2023')+'''.</p>
<p>The lesson is not that correlation is a bad score. It is that the data the score is computed on is part of the claim. The course scores on every token of the held-out split, which is the “random-only” regime and the stricter one, and reports the base rate so you can see how sparse the target is. Anthropic’s dictionary-learning paper notes the same choice: they sampled “across the full range of activation intervals” and distinguish this from Bills et al.’s top-and-random mixture '''+cite('monosemantic')+'''.</p>
<table><tr><th>Score</th><th>Measures</th><th>Blind to</th></tr>
<tr><td>Correlation on all held-out tokens</td><td>Linear match of rule and activation across the whole distribution</td><td>Magnitude errors within “fires”; dominated by the sparse strong tail</td></tr>
<tr><td>Correlation on top-and-random excerpts</td><td>Whether the strongest feature is captured without being too broad</td><td>Everything in the middle of the distribution</td></tr>
<tr><td>Precision and recall of “fires”</td><td>Sufficiency and necessity of the rule as a classifier</td><td>Magnitudes; needs a threshold for “fires”</td></tr>
<tr><td>Downstream KL after substitution (lesson 13)</td><td>Whether the simulation errors matter to the model’s output</td><td>Needs a forward pass per substitution; small per neuron</td></tr></table>
<h2 id="lab">The lab</h2>
<p><code>labs/explanation_score.py</code> asks for <code>split_chunks</code>, <code>rule_predictions</code> (regex or predicate), <code>score_explanation</code>, the two controls, and a <code>verdict</code>. The tests build a synthetic activation matrix where unit 0 fires on digits and unit 1 on capitalised words, and check that the true rule is demonstrated, that the capital rule applied to the digit unit is not, and that the random-unit control catches the case where the rule is real but belongs to a different unit.</p>
<div class="evals"><span class="label">In interpretability work</span><p>Report four numbers with every explanation: held-out score, random-unit control percentile, shuffled-label control percentile, base rate. A score without its controls is a story; a score without its base rate is unreadable.</p></div>
<div class="pitfall"><span class="label">Pitfall</span><p>Scoring on the screening split and calling it validation. The screening correlation is the best of thousands; the drop to held-out was small here because the digit rule is nearly exact, but for a weaker rule the drop is the whole story. The eighth row of lesson 05’s table shows one that fell from 0.651 to 0.528.</p></div>''',
 'An explanation scores r = 0.9 on top-and-random excerpts and r = 0.3 on random-only excerpts. What does the neuron probably look like?',
 'It probably has a clean strong tail (the top excerpts are captured) and a broad or differently-shaped weak region that the explanation does not predict: on random text the explanation either fires where the neuron is quiet or misses the many weak firings. Bills et al. report this pattern directly: correlation scores of 0.9 still gave average ablation scores of 0.3 on random-only text. Whether the weak region matters is the Gao–Cammarata disagreement; that it exists is what the two scores tell you.',
 lab(['python labs/reference/neuron_score.py --rule digit',"python -m unittest discover -s labs -p 'test_explanation_score.py' -v"],
 'Run the digit scoring, then implement <code>labs/explanation_score.py</code> until its tests pass. Then write a rule of your own for L2/2928 that is stricter than “after a full stop or newline” (for example, excluding tokens after a closing quote) and score it with the reference script’s machinery.',
 'A stricter rule can raise precision and lower recall at once; whether r rises depends on which error was larger. Record both rules’ held-out r and both controls in your protocol notes. The point of the exercise is to feel the score move for a reason you can name.'), ['bills2023','huang2023','monosemantic']),

lesson(5,1,'controls','Controls: what each one rules out, with worked numbers',
 'Random unit, shuffled labels, baseline explanation, and the held-out split itself. Each closes a different door, and the full table from the authoring run shows them at work.',
 '''<h2 id="the-table">All eight rules at once</h2>
<p>The same screening and scoring procedure, run for every rule the course defines. Nothing was tuned between rules; the pre-registered verdict is the same for all.</p>
'''+transcript('''         rule layer neuron  train r held-out r  rand95  shuf95 |rand|max  base rate  verdict
        digit     0    908    0.979      0.978   0.116   0.040     0.334      0.029  demonstrated
      capital     0   1102    0.757      0.727   0.115   0.044     0.271      0.076  demonstrated
      newline     0    504    0.988      0.995   0.131   0.044     0.335      0.044  demonstrated
        punct     9    788    0.919      0.918   0.187   0.044     0.558      0.117  demonstrated
 after_period     2   2928    0.860      0.845   0.125   0.042     0.560      0.103  demonstrated
        quote     0    121    0.777      0.828   0.069   0.044     0.512      0.016  demonstrated
          the     0    132    0.996      0.997   0.131   0.041     0.335      0.059  demonstrated
   space_word     1   1034    0.651      0.528   0.231   0.042     0.372      0.700  demonstrated''')+'''
<p>Eight rules, eight neurons found by exhaustive screening, eight held-out scores above 0.5 and above both controls. Before reading that as “GPT-2 small has a neuron for everything,” read the controls, because they are what make the table mean anything.</p>
<h2 id="random-unit">The random-unit control</h2>
<p>Score the same rule against 200 randomly chosen other neurons of the same layer. The 95th percentile (<code>rand95</code>) answers: how well does this rule fit a neuron that was not selected for it? For most rules it is 0.07–0.19, which is the floor set by the rule’s own structure: any rule correlates slightly with many neurons because the tokens it marks are not random tokens. For <code>space_word</code> (a token that starts with a space and is alphabetic, 70% of all tokens) the floor is 0.231, because such a broad rule is weakly correlated with almost everything.</p>
<p>The maximum over the 200 (<code>|rand|max</code>) is the more sobering column. For <code>after_period</code> the single most correlated random neuron reached 0.560, for <code>punct</code> 0.558, for <code>quote</code> 0.512. That is what the tail of 200 draws looks like, and it is why the verdict uses the 95th percentile rather than the maximum: the maximum of a null distribution grows with the number of draws, and a threshold set at the maximum would be a threshold set by how many controls you ran. The <a href="../../../statistics/statistics-tut/index.html">statistics course</a>’s multiple-comparisons lesson covers the general case; here the practical rule is to fix the number of control draws in the protocol and compare against a percentile.</p>
<h2 id="shuffled">The shuffled-label control</h2>
<p>Permute the rule’s predictions 500 times and score each against the real neuron. This answers: what does a rule with the right base rate but no content score on this neuron? The 95th percentile is 0.04–0.05 for every rule, which is the sampling floor for 1,536 tokens. If a held-out score were 0.1, this control would tell you it is above chance; it would not tell you it is interesting. The two controls together bracket the score from both sides: the random unit asks whether the rule is special to this neuron; the shuffle asks whether the neuron’s response is special to this rule.</p>
<h2 id="baseline">The baseline explanation</h2>
<p>A third control is a competing explanation rather than a null. For L2/2928, “after a full stop or newline” scores 0.845. A baseline rule that explains some of the same tokens, say “token is capitalised” (0.727 for its own neuron, L0/1102), can be scored on L2/2928 too. If the baseline scored nearly as high, the specific claim would not have earned its specificity. The reference script does not run this for you; it is the first thing to add in the capstone, and the protocol lab’s <code>judge()</code> accepts any number of named controls for exactly this reason.</p>
<h2 id="held-out">The split as a control on yourself</h2>
<p>Compare the <code>train r</code> and <code>held-out r</code> columns. For sharp rules the drop is negligible (digit: 0.979 to 0.978). For <code>space_word</code> it is large: 0.651 to 0.528. The screening score is the maximum over 36,864 candidates and includes whatever fit the chosen neuron had to the particular screening chunks; the held-out score is what survives. The drop is a direct measurement of selection bias, and it is largest for the broadest rule because a broad rule has the most ways to fit by accident. Had the threshold been 0.6, <code>space_word</code> would have passed on screening and failed on held-out, which is the correct outcome.</p>
<h2 id="what-the-table-shows">What the table does and does not show</h2>
<p>It shows that for eight simple token-level rules, GPT-2 small has at least one MLP neuron whose activation correlates with the rule on held-out prose from this corpus, at a level far above either null. Six of the eight live in layer 0, where neurons are close to token identity, and the deepest, punctuation at layer 9, is the one whose rule needs no context either. The table does not show that these neurons “are for” these properties (lesson 10 is about that), that they do nothing else (a rule explains the variance it explains; 0.845² is 71%), or that the result transfers to text unlike the corpus. Each of those is a further claim with its own test.</p>
<div class="evals"><span class="label">In interpretability work</span><p>Huang et al. used a random-pairing control for exactly this purpose and found “the random baseline has a F1 score of zero” '''+cite('huang2023')+''' against GPT-4 explanations’ 0.56. The number that matters in any such comparison is the gap, and the gap is only meaningful when the control was run the same way as the claim.</p></div>
<div class="pitfall"><span class="label">Pitfall</span><p>Running controls after seeing the score and stopping when they look low enough. Fix the number of random units and shuffles in the protocol. The reference script uses 200 and 500 and prints both percentiles whatever they are.</p></div>''',
 'Rule A scores 0.60 held-out with rand95 = 0.12. Rule B scores 0.60 held-out with rand95 = 0.55. Both pass the threshold. Which neuron have you learned more about?',
 'Rule A’s neuron. The gap between score and random-unit control is what measures the specificity of the rule to the neuron: for B, more than half of the neurons in the layer fit the rule almost as well, so the rule describes something about the layer (or about the rule’s own breadth) rather than about the neuron. B is not a false result, but it is a weak explanation of this particular unit, and the write-up should say so.',
 lab(['python labs/reference/neuron_score.py --all'],
 'Run the full table. Then pick the row with the largest screening-to-held-out drop and the row with the largest |rand|max, and write one sentence for each explaining which control caught what.',
 'The largest drop is space_word, the broadest rule, caught by the held-out split. The largest |rand|max is after_period (0.560): one other layer-2 neuron tracks sentence starts at about two thirds of the chosen neuron’s score, and the 95th percentile, not the maximum, kept the verdict stable. If your run differs in a column, check the transcript header for versions; seeds are fixed.'), ['huang2023','bills2023']),
]
